Conclusion
In this lab, you built a complete Enterprise Integration Patterns pipeline by connecting Apache Camel to Apache ActiveMQ: you configured a persistent, multi-protocol broker; wrote a chain of routes that generate, process, fulfill, and notify on orders using the Message Channel and Pipes-and-Filters patterns; and layered in a Dead Letter Channel with bounded redelivery to handle failures gracefully. You then validated the system from multiple angles, using the ActiveMQ web console to observe live queue depths, shell scripts to drive error and load scenarios, manual persistence checks to confirm messages survive consumer downtime, and JUnit 5 with camel-test-junit5 to lock in the processing and error-handling logic with fast, broker-independent automated tests. Together these exercises demonstrate the core reliability guarantees that make message-oriented middleware valuable in production systems: durable storage, automatic retry, and dead-letter isolation of poison messages.

To clean up your environment, stop the Camel application with Ctrl+C in its terminal, then stop ActiveMQ with Ctrl+C in the broker's console terminal (or ./bin/activemq stop if it was started as a background service). You can optionally remove the KahaDB data directory under ActiveMQ's data folder if you want a completely fresh broker state for future runs, and remove ~/camel-activemq-lab if you no longer need the project files.

As a next step, consider extending this lab by adding a Content-Based Router that splits orders by product category into separate processing queues, introducing a Splitter/Aggregator pair to batch orders by customer before fulfillment, or replacing the REST-based test scripts with a proper JMS producer client for more realistic load generation.
